  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 908 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nA subtle upper shortwave is shown to traverse the region Sunday \nnight into Monday, followed by weak, brief ridging through Tuesday. \nThe main thing that we'll need to watch will come by mid to late \nweek, as a deepening trough over the central CONUS digs into the \nSoutheast. At the surface, a cold front is slated to sweep over the \nregion Sunday into Monday morning. Then, high pressure will largely \ntake hold and persist through the week. The exception will be on \nWednesday when what looks like an inverted trough develops over the \nSoutheast and up into the Ohio Valley. There still remains quite a \nbit of disagreement with regards to the evolution and timing of these \nsynoptic and surface features, as well as any instability and shear \nfor storm development, so stay tuned for updates!\n\nFor now, expect low to medium chances (10-40%) of showers and \nperhaps a storm Sunday evening through Sunday night. Confidence is \nlow with regards to storm potential at the moment, since instability \nand shear appear to be low. This will be monitored. After drier \nweather (little to no rain) Monday through Tuesday, rain chances \nreturn for Wednesday (30-60%) - this the main system we mentioned \nthat are monitoring in the extended term. Make sure to keep an eye on\nthe forecast over the coming days for updates, especially with \nholiday travel for Thanksgiving!\n\nAs for temperatures, a cooling trend will comprise most of the long \nterm period. Highs are forecast to begin in the upper 50s to mid 60s\nbut by Thursday are projected to merely top out in the upper 40s to \nlower 50s. Lows follow a similar pattern, starting out in the upper \n40s to lower 50s Sunday night and drop into the lower to mid 30s by \nWednesday night.",
